        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Content -->
  <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-6 mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Physical Sizing: C9 vs C7 Dimensions</h2>
    <p>
      The primary distinction between C9 and C7 bulbs is scale:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>C9 Bulbs:</strong> Measure approximately 2-1/4 inches tall by 1-1/4 inches wide at the globe. They use an <strong>E17 Intermediate base</strong> (17mm diameter thread). Their large physical footprint makes them visible from 200+ feet away, making them the universal choice for roof peaks, dormers, and tall fascia boards.</li>
      <li><strong>C7 Bulbs:</strong> Measure approximately 1-1/2 inches tall by 7/8 inch wide. They utilize an <strong>E12 Candelabra base</strong> (12mm diameter thread—the same size as standard interior chandelier bulbs). Because of their delicate proportion, C7 bulbs are preferred for ground pathway stakes, column wraps, window trim, and low front porch railings.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Socket Spacing Rules: 12-inch vs 15-inch vs 6-inch</h2>
    <p>
      Commercial SPT-2 wire spools come pre-molded with sockets spaced at specific intervals:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>12-inch Spacing (Standard Commercial):</strong> The golden standard for residential rooflines. It provides a tight, cohesive ribbon of light from the street without crowding or twisting bulbs together.</li>
      <li><strong>15-inch Spacing:</strong> Ideal for large commercial buildings, expansive rural rooflines, or budget-conscious installations where long distances must be covered with fewer total bulbs.</li>
      <li><strong>6-inch or 8-inch Spacing:</strong> Primarily used with C7 bulbs along sidewalks, flower bed borders, or tight architectural curves where high light density is required.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Comparative Table -->
