    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">How to Install Vampire Plugs on SPT-2 Commercial Wire</h2>
    <p>
      Gilbert "vampire" plugs allow installers to customize strings without cutting, splicing, or wire-nutting. Follow this precise installation sequence:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Cut your 18 AWG SPT-2 wire cleanly across with sharp wire cutters (do not strip any insulation).</li>
      <li>Identify the <strong>ribbed / neutral conductor</strong> on the side of the wire casing and align it with the wider, polarized silver blade on the vampire connector.</li>
      <li>Place the smooth / hot conductor on the narrow brass pin.</li>
      <li>Slide the plastic locking cap firmly into place until it clicks, forcing the copper vampire teeth through the PVC insulation directly into the copper wire core.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Pro Field Note -->
  <div class="mb-10">
    <FieldNote
      title="SPT-1 vs SPT-2 Wire Insulation Thickness"
      trade="Commercial Holiday Lighting"
      content="Always ensure your vampire connectors match your wire type. SPT-1 has a 0.030-inch PVC insulation wall, whereas SPT-2 has a thicker 0.045-inch heavy-duty insulation wall designed for freezing outdoor exposure. If you attempt to press SPT-1 vampire plugs onto thicker SPT-2 wire, the plastic clips will crack or the teeth will fail to penetrate cleanly, resulting in high-resistance connections and melting plugs."
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Critical Costly Mistakes Box -->
  <div class="mb-12">
    <CostlyMistakes
      projectType="Holiday Lighting Electrical Setup"
      mistakes={[
        {
          mistake: "Reversing Polarity on Gilbert Vampire Plugs",
          impact: "Connecting the hot wire to the outer screw shell of the socket creates a lethal 120V shock hazard for anyone changing a burnt-out bulb while standing on an aluminum ladder.",
          prevention: "Always verify that the ribbed neutral wire connects to the wide polarized blade, which feeds the outer screw shell, keeping the hot wire on the recessed center foot."
        },
        {
          mistake: "Overloading 18 AWG Wire Past 7 Amps (840 Watts)",
          impact: "Exceeding ampacity causes resistive heating along the copper conductor, melting the wire insulation and setting dry roofing cedar shingles or fascia trim on fire.",
